    if not isinstance(x, str):\\n                    raise Bad(f\\\"{where}: entries_b64[{i}] must be a string\\\")\\n                try:\\n                    base64.b64decode(x, validate=True)\\n                except ValueError:\\n                    raise Bad(f\\\"{where}: entries_b64[{i}] must be base64\\\")\"}]}"}}
{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":13244,"output":3528,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2943,"totalTokens":16772,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":true}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Let me understand the task:\n\n1. I need to add a function `_orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None` to `record/record.py`\n2. The function should validate that an orphan entry's hidden entries match the attested range\n3. Wire it into wherever orphans are checked\n4. All existing tests must pass\n\nLet me analyze the code:\n\n- An orphan entry has a body with `statement_b64` which is a base64-encoded canonical entry bytes\n- The `statement_b64` contains a DSSE envelope with a predicate that has `from_seq`, `to_seq`, `prev_sha256`, `head_sha256`\n- The orphan entry also has `entries_b64` which is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes\n\nWait, looking at the code more carefully:\n\nIn `check_body` for orphan:\n```python\nelse:  # orphan: explains a Rekor attestation by the writer that the ledger doesn't hold\n    allowed = {\"by\", \"log_index\", \"reason\", \"statement_b64\"}\n```\n\nSo the orphan body has `statement_b64`, not `entries_b64`. But the task says `orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes.\n\nWait, let me re-read the task:\n- `orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes (as produced by `R.canonical(entry)`).\n\nBut looking at the current code, the orphan body only allows `by`, `log_index`, `reason`, `statement_b64`. There's no `entries_b64` field.\n\nLet me look at the monitor command to see how orphans are checked:\n\n```python\no = explained[idx]\nbody = json.loads(base64.b64decode(v[\"body\"]))\nstmt = base64.b64decode(o[\"body\"][\"statement_b64\"])\nif body.get(\"kind\") != \"dsse\" or body[\"spec\"][\"payloadHash\"][\"value\"] != hashlib.sha256(stmt).hexdigest():\n    bad.append(f\"orphan entry {o['seq']}: its statement isn't the one Rekor holds for log index {idx}\")\n    continue\npred = (parse_json(stmt, f\"orphan entry {o['seq']} statement\") or {}).get(\"predicate\") or {}\nhead = pred.get(\"head_sha256\")\nif head in published and head not in current:\n    bad.append(f\"orphan entry {o['seq']}: the attested head {head} was published on {a.git_rev} and later removed\")\n    continue\n```\n\nSo the monitor checks:\n1. The orphan's statement_b64 matches what Rekor has\n2. The predicate's head_sha256 wasn't published and then removed\n\nBut the task says an orphan could \"hide an attested entry, such as a failing verdict\". The orphan needs to publish the entries it hid.\n\nLooking at the task description again: \"If a run attests a range and then fails, an 'orphan' record explains that attestation. A reviewer found that an orphan explained only by its statement could hide an attested entry, such as a failing verdict.\"\n\nSo the orphan should include the entries that were attested but not written to the ledger. These would be in `entries_b64` in the orphan body.\n\nLet me check if there's any mention of `entries_b64` in the code... I don't see it. The task says to add support for it.\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Allow `entries_b64` in the orphan body schema\n2. Implement `_orphan_entries_problem` to validate the entries\n3. Wire it into the monitor check\n\nLet me first understand what the predicate structure looks like. From `range_predicate`:\n```python\ndef range_predicate(chain, from_seq, to_seq):\n    return {\"from_seq\": from_seq, \"to_seq\": to_seq,\n            \"prev_sha256\": chain[from_seq - 2][1] if from_seq > 1 else None, \"head_sha256\": chain[to_seq - 1][1]}\n```\n\nSo the predicate has:\n- `from_seq`: starting sequence number\n- `to_seq`: ending sequence number\n- `prev_sha256`: hash of entry before the range (or None if from_seq == 1)\n- `head_sha256`: hash of the last entry in the range\n\nThe orphan's `entries_b64` should contain exactly `to_seq - from_seq + 1` entries, where:\n- The first entry's `prev` field equals `pred[\"prev_sha256\"]`\n- Each subsequent entry's `prev` field equals the previous entry's sha256\n- The last entry's sha256 equals `pred[\"head_sha256\"]`\n\nNow let me implement this.\n\nFirst, I need to update the orphan schema to allow `entries_b64`:\n```python\nallowed = {\"by\", \"log_index\", \"reason\", \"statement_b64\", \"entries_b64\"}\n```\n\nThen implement `_orphan_entries_problem`:\n```python\ndef _orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None:\n    entries_b64 = orphan[\"body\"].get(\"entries_b64\")\n    if not entries_b64:\n        return \"missing entries_b64\"\n    # ... validation logic\n```\n\nWait, the task says \"Return `None` when the decoded entries are exactly the attested range\". So if `entries_b64` is missing, that's a problem. But the task also says the function should return specific error messages.\n\nLet me re-read the requirements:\n- Return `None` when the decoded entries are exactly the attested range\n- Otherwise return a short problem string:\n  - one containing the word `range` when the count is wrong;\n  - one containing `chain` when an entry's `prev` doesn't link;\n  - one containing `head` when the last hash doesn't match `head_sha256`.\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Decode each entry from base64\n2. Check the count matches `to_seq - from_seq + 1`\n3. Check the chain linkage\n4. Check the final hash\n\nLet me write the function:\n\n```python\ndef _orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None:\n    entries_b64 = orphan[\"body\"].get(\"entries_b64\")\n    if not isinstance(entries_b64, list):\n        return \"range: missing or invalid entries_b64\"\n    \n    from_seq = pred.get(\"from_seq\")\n    to_seq = pred.get(\"to_seq\")\n    if not isinstance(from_seq, int) or not isinstance(to_seq, int):\n        return \"range: invalid predicate\"\n    \n    expected_count = to_seq - from_seq + 1\n    if len(entries_b64) != expected_count:\n        return f\"range: expected {expected_count} entries, got {len(entries_b64)}\"\n    \n    prev_sha256 = pred.get(\"prev_sha256\")\n    head_sha256 = pred.get(\"head_sha256\")\n    \n    last_sha = None\n    for i, entry_b64 in enumerate(entries_b64):\n        try:\n            entry_raw = base64.b64decode(entry_b64, validate=True)\n        except ValueError:\n            return f\"chain: entry {i} not valid base64\"\n        \n        try:\n            entry = parse_json(entry_raw, f\"orphan entry {i}\")\n        except Bad as ex:\n            return f\"chain: entry {i} invalid ({ex})\"\n        \n        # Check it's a valid entry structure\n        if not isinstance(entry, dict) or set(entry) != {\"seq\", \"prev\", \"kind\", \"written_at\", \"body\"}:\n            return f\"chain: entry {i} malformed\"\n        \n        # Check seq matches\n        if entry[\"seq\"] != from_seq + i:\n            return f\"chain: entry {i} seq {entry['seq']} != expected {from_seq + i}\"\n        \n        # Check prev links\n        if entry[\"prev\"] != prev_sha256:\n            return f\"chain: entry {i} prev {entry['prev']!r} != expected {prev_sha256!r}\"\n        \n        # Compute this entry's sha256\n        last_sha = hashlib.sha256(entry_raw).hexdigest()\n        prev_sha256 = last_sha\n    \n    # Check final hash matches head_sha256\n    if last_sha != head_sha256:\n        return f\"head: last entry sha256 {last_sha} != predicate head_sha256 {head_sha256}\"\n    \n    return None\n```\n\nNow I need to wire this into the monitor check. Looking at the monitor code:\n\n```python\no = explained[idx]\nbody = json.loads(base64.b64decode(v[\"body\"]))\nstmt = base64.b64decode(o[\"body\"][\"statement_b64\"])\nif body.get(\"kind\") != \"dsse\" or body[\"spec\"][\"payloadHash\"][\"value\"] != hashlib.sha256(stmt).hexdigest():\n    bad.append(f\"orphan entry {o['seq']}: its statement isn't the one Rekor holds for log index {idx}\")\n    continue\npred = (parse_json(stmt, f\"orphan entry {o['seq']} statement\") or {}).get(\"predicate\") or {}\nhead = pred.get(\"head_sha256\")\nif head in published and head not in current:\n    bad.append(f\"orphan entry {o['seq']}: the attested head {head} was published on {a.git_rev} and later removed\")\n    continue\norphans.append(dict(row, explained_by_entry=o[\"seq\"], attested_range=f\"{pred.get('from_seq')}-{pred.get('to_seq')}\"))\n```\n\nI need to add a check after getting `pred`:\n\n```python\nproblem = _orphan_entries_problem(o, pred)\nif problem:\n    bad.append(f\"orphan entry {o['seq']}: {problem}\")\n    continue\n```\n\nAlso, I should update the schema to allow `entries_b64` in orphan bodies.\n\nLet me also check if there's any validation needed when ingesting an orphan. Looking at `check_body` for orphan:\n\n```python\nelse:  # orphan: explains a Rekor attestation by the writer that the ledger doesn't hold\n    allowed = {\"by\", \"log_index\", \"reason\", \"statement_b64\"}\n    if _need(body, \"log_index\", int, where) < 0 or not _need(body, \"reason\", str, where).strip():\n        raise Bad(f\"{where}: orphan needs log_index >= 0 and a reason\")\n    try:  # the orphaned attestation's exact signed statement; monitor checks it against Rekor's payload hash\n        base64.b64decode(_need(body, \"statement_b64\", str, where), validate=True)\n    except ValueError:\n        raise Bad(f\"{where}: statement_b64 must be base64\")\n```\n\nI should add `entries_b64` to allowed and optionally validate it's a list of base64 strings. But the task says to wire it into \"wherever orphans are checked\", which seems to be primarily the monitor. The ingest might not need to validate the entries_b64 content, just allow the field.\n\nActually, wait. The task says \"Wire it into wherever orphans are checked, so an orphan without valid hidden entries is reported.\" The monitor is the main place where orphans are checked. But maybe we should also check during verify? Let me check the verify command:\n\n```python\ndef cmd_verify(a):\n    chain, verdict_of = load_chain(a.ledger)\n    today = datetime.date.today().isoformat()\n    plans = [e for e, _ in chain if e[\"kind\"] == \"plan\"]\n    open_ = [e[\"seq\"] for e in plans if e[\"seq\"] not in verdict_of]\n    overdue = [e[\"seq\"] for e in plans if e[\"seq\"] not in verdict_of and e[\"body\"].get(\"verdict_due\", \"9999-12-31\") < today]\n    print(json.dumps({\"entries\": len(chain), \"head_seq\": len(chain), \"head_sha256\": chain[-1][1] if chain else None,\n                      \"plans\": len(plans), \"verdicts\": len(verdict_of),\n                      \"orphans_explained\": sum(e[\"kind\"] == \"orphan\" for e, _ in chain),\n                      \"open_plans\": open_, \"overdue_plans\": overdue}, indent=1))\n    return 0\n```\n\nThe verify command just counts orphans. It doesn't validate their content. The monitor is the command that validates orphans against Rekor.\n\nSo I think the main place to wire it is in `cmd_monitor`.\n\nLet me now implement the changes.\n\nFirst, let me update the `check_body` function to allow `entries_b64` in orphan bodies.\n\nThen add the `_orphan_entries_problem` function.\n\nThen wire it into `cmd_monitor`.\n\nLet me do this step by step."}}
