ex\": 12345, \"reason\": \"run failed after attesting\",\n                                        \"statement_b64\": \"e30=\"})\n    self.assertEqual(self.run_cmd(\"ingest\")[0], 0)\n    self.assertEqual(json.loads(self.run_cmd(\"verify\")[1])[\"orphans_explained\"], 1)\n```\n\nThis test doesn't include `entries_b64` and it passes. The ingest command allows orphans without `entries_b64` (it's optional). The monitor command is where the validation happens. Since the test doesn't run the monitor command, it's fine.\n\nLet me also check if there are any other places where orphans are checked. Looking at the code:\n1. `ingest` - validates the orphan schema, now allows `entries_b64`\n2. `verify` - just counts orphans\n3. `monitor` - validates orphans against Rekor, now also validates entries_b64\n\nThat seems complete. Let me also run a quick manual test to make sure the function works as expected.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"All tests pass. Let me also verify the function works correctly by writing a quick test to make sure the logic is correct. But first, let me check if there's any edge case I might have missed.\\n\\nLet me review the function once more:\\n1. It checks that entries_b64 is a list\\n2. It checks the count matches the expected range\\n3. It checks each entry's prev field chains correctly\\n4. It checks the final hash matches head_sha256\\n\\nThe error messages contain \\\"range\\\", \\\"chain\\\", or \\\"head\\\" as required.\\n\\nLet me also make sure the function handles the case where entries_b64 is not present or empty correctly. The function returns \\\"range: missing or invalid entries_b64\\\" in that case.\\n\\nWait, there's an issue - the function checks `if not isinstance(entries_b64, list):` but `orphan[\\\"body\\\"].get(\\\"entries_b64\\\")` would return `None` if the key doesn't exist, and `None` is not a list, so it would correctly return the error. But what if the list is empty? An empty list is a list, so